<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacques Dyel du Parquet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1606">1606</a>–<a href="/years/1658">1658</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacques Dyel du Parquet was a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-belain-desnambuc">Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc</a> and governor of Martinique from 1637. In 1649 he landed settlers on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a> and built a fort at the site of St George's, and in 1650 he sent settlers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-lucia">Saint Lucia</a>. That year he bought Martinique, Grenada and Saint Lucia from the bankrupt Company of the Isles of America, ruling them as proprietor until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacques-dyel-du-parquet.jpg" alt="Jacques Dyel du Parquet" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dyel_du_Parquet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jacques Dyel du Parquet was born in 1606 in Cailleville, Normandy, the son of Pierre Dyel, lord of Vaudroques, and Adrienne de Blain. His uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-belain-desnambuc">Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc</a> led the French settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> in 1627, and du Parquet's brother died of wounds after a failed attack on the Spanish fleet of Fadrique de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> there in 1629. In 1635 d'Esnambuc sent men to Martinique and founded Saint-Pierre; when its commander Dupont was captured by the Spanish, du Parquet was sent to replace him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Officially named governor for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compagnie-des-iles-de-lamerique">Compagnie des Îles de l'Amérique</a> in 1637, du Parquet settled at Saint-Pierre and built a military camp that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-de-france">Fort-de-France</a>. That year he agreed a truce with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago">Island Caribs</a> leaving the French the leeward coast. The colony grew from 700 men in 1639 to 1,000 in 1640. In the quarrel between the lieutenant general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phillippe-de-longvilliers-de-poincy">Phillippe de Longvilliers de Poincy</a> and his named successor Noël Patrocle de Thoisy, du Parquet sided with Thoisy and was taken captive by Poincy; his wife, Marie Bonnard, led the demand for his release.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenada">Grenada</a> his 203 men founded Fort Annunciation in March 1649 and partitioned the island with Chief Kairouane, but fighting began that November and lasted until 1654. The purchase of the islands was contracted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 27 September 1650 for 41,500 livres, and in October 1651 the King appointed him governor and lieutenant general over them. In 1654 Caribs and rebellious slaves besieged his fort, La Montagne, until four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ships landed 300 soldiers. The Portuguese Jews they carried from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> set up the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> refineries on Martinique. He sold Grenada in 1655 and made peace with the Caribs on 18 October 1657.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Du Parquet died at Saint-Pierre on 3 January 1658, aged 52. His widow governed as regent for their eldest son, Louis, whom the King named governor of Martinique and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-lucia">Saint Lucia</a>, with du Parquet's brother Adrien Dyel de Vaudroques acting for him. In 1664 the King revoked the old company's sales and created the Compagnie des Indes Occidentales, and on 14 August 1665 the heirs were forced to sell Martinique and Saint Lucia to it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacques Dyel du Parquet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Jacques Dyel du Parquet</b>, governor of Martinique, bought rights to Grenada and landed with settlers in 1649, founding a fort at the site of St George's.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-grenada">French Grenada</a></li><li><blockquote>After failed English attempts in 1605 and 1639, settlers sent by <b>Jacques Dyel du Parquet</b> of Martinique established a French post on Saint Lucia in 1650 and reached an accommodation with the Kalinago.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-saint-lucia">French Saint Lucia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-pierre-martinique">Saint-Pierre</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.74,-61.18&amp;year=1639" aria-label="Saint-Pierre on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Jacques Dyel du Parquet"><image href="/maps/globes/g30000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4903 3753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4903 3753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=14.74,-61.18&amp;year=1639">What was here in 1639? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
